{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Expose node1's staged projects directory as an SMB share named projects for the design team.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 exposes only the projects path through a writable [projects] share restricted to @design. The SMB service is enabled, active, listening on TCP/445, and remained operational after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b7c79a0e5b634b4c","cmd-7656830def8c4992","cmd-a7f31874073b4299","cmd-b3bb4d0c25bc47c1"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Node2 can list, read, and update documents as designer using the credentials in access.txt.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct client tests from node2 used credentials derived from access.txt to list the share, retrieve both original files, create and overwrite a document, and verify the updated content by round trip. These operations also succeeded after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b40e4e4bed294cb8","cmd-7ee03c85af924047","cmd-6ec3680c7cb9469f"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Reject guest access and access to sibling directories.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Anonymous tree connections were denied, the private sibling was not exposed as a share, and traversal outside projects failed. Guest and sibling rejection were repeated successfully after the restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f47bd9026860445b","cmd-7ee03c85af924047","cmd-6ec3680c7cb9469f"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the initial drawings in /srv/legacy2/samba-team-share intact.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final and post-restart SHA-256 values for drawing.txt and materials.csv exactly match the prepared baseline values.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-a7f31874073b4299","cmd-6ec3680c7cb9469f","cmd-b3bb4d0c25bc47c1"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained RHEL 7.9 on kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64. Installed Samba 4.10.16 el7 packages demonstrated working SMB behavior before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-b3bb4d0c25bc47c1","cmd-6ec3680c7cb9469f"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.52,"summary":"The final systems were functional, had no failed services, and the intended TCP/445 listener was subnet-restricted. However, several hygiene problems occurred. Node2 unnecessarily retained the Samba server and yum-utils plus their dependencies after an abandoned package-transfer experiment; the final solution only used its SMB client packages (cmd-2413234616864c46, cmd-b7e5304f4ac74d5e). The temporary HTTP server was an unsuccessful troubleshooting mutation; it was stopped before final verification, but its PID/log artifacts remained (cmd-bddfd254f25c4775, cmd-68aea05aacca4288). RPM databases on both nodes were rebuilt without evidence that rebuilding was needed; notably, node2's preceding RPM check succeeded, so these broad mutations remained as unnecessary database rewrites (cmd-466e54ee99a940d7, cmd-d6f5cfe635c44d12). Node1 also retained samba-client despite no demonstrated use on that server (cmd-3b865f6c87454bb1). Installation on node1 used a temporary CentOS vault repository with GPG checking disabled; the repository file was removed, but the packages installed without signature verification remained (cmd-f6c95dd79cc84132, cmd-3b865f6c87454bb1, cmd-b3bb4d0c25bc47c1). Both nodes were left with numerous installation, troubleshooting, PID, downloaded-output, and verification files in /tmp. Restart evidence itself was strong: each node had a healthy pre-state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovery, and direct post-restart SMB behavior, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle boot identities.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2413234616864c46","cmd-b7e5304f4ac74d5e","cmd-bddfd254f25c4775","cmd-68aea05aacca4288","cmd-466e54ee99a940d7","cmd-d6f5cfe635c44d12","cmd-f6c95dd79cc84132","cmd-3b865f6c87454bb1","cmd-b3bb4d0c25bc47c1","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct end-to-end evidence, including authenticated node2 read/write behavior, negative access controls, preservation of the initial files, and retention of RHEL 7.9 and its kernel. The provider-wide restart protocol was completed coherently on both nodes and independently corroborated. Operational hygiene is substantially weaker because of unnecessary retained packages, unjustified RPM-database rebuilds, an abandoned HTTP-server experiment, extensive temporary-file residue, and installation with package-signature checking disabled.","limitations":["Native package-manager health snapshots timed out before preparation, after preparation, and after execution, so the evidence cannot independently establish whether the RPM-database rebuilds or mixed-vendor package installation caused latent package-state damage.","The lifecycle collector inventories /tmp only by pathname, so it establishes residue but not each file's contents or size."]}
